# Q: does the ship arrive, and does it arrive the same way twice?
#
# The phase 0 gate in probe form. A hundred-tonne crewed hull launches two kilometres down a
# station's corridor and the world runs at 120 Hz until it is either captured or past.
#
#   dotnet run -c Release --project src/SolSystem.Probe -- \
#       --probe tools/probe/docking.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/docking.txt
#
# The hash at the end is the reason this exists. Two runs have to produce the same one, and if
# they do not then something in the simulation is reading a clock, an iteration order or a
# float -- which it would also do in a played game, where it is far harder to notice.
#
# The guidance law lives in SolSystem.Core, not here: a probe is the worst place to develop a
# controller, because every fix has to be re-derived without tests. This script exercises it
# and checks the outcome.
#
# Note the length. A two-kilometre approach at four milligee takes about a thousand seconds,
# most of it the two half-minute reversals, so the run is 250 000 ticks and a short probe
# would report a ship still on its way as a failure.

launch Meridian 2000 0.0
phase
range

# Half a minute.
advance 3600
range
closing
ship

# The approach, to the capture envelope.
advance 250000
phase
range
closing
ship

# Contact comes at about 131 600 ticks — the same clock the tests pin — and the hold
# latches there. What happens after is honest but bare: the law stops flying, nothing
# models the latches taking the ship yet, and the 0.15 m/s residual carries it gently
# through the port and away. The run continues for two more minutes because that is
# what catches a hold that thrusts: one version did, and threw the ship through the
# port and forty metres a second out the other side. So the checks are that contact
# happened and that the law then left the ship alone.
expect "the latches have it" phase 3 exactly
expect "a held ship is not thrown anywhere" speed 0.2 less

hash docking
